## Static three-anchor audit

Three old contact neighbors of a loaded degree-three vertex have that
boundary vertex as their unique unit circumcenter; preserving all three
cannot produce an interior replacement. Isolates can also be chosen
arbitrarily generically inside their finite strict-slack neighborhood,
without changing C, H, D, cardinality or lexicographic normalization.
Avoiding finitely many unit-circumcircle polynomial equations forces every
static surrounding singleton anchor to be an actual core vertex. Thus
isolated fan blockers cannot be promoted to exact static anchors solely
from inverse-D near-unit proximity. A new core triple with a unit
circumcenter, clearance from all other retained points and no induced
triangle remains unproved. Collective packets and motions creating new
anchor equalities are explicitly outside this exclusion.

Composed static anchors with the canonical short-wall winding proof.
Weak clearance is impossible for at most five unit sides; for six sides
it forces a regular unit hexagon and its center, whose insertion creates
unit triangles. Thus preserved pentagon/hexagon faces cannot host a
triangle-free exact-contact packet either. Deleted wall guards invalidate
this particular winding exclusion; strict sub-D competitors may contain
triangles. Neither distinction is suppressed.

Further composition: canonical midpoint concentration puts the actual
diameter normals of any loaded unit-circle cluster in a short interval.
Its hull chain cannot surround the unit center. Together with generic
isolate avoidance, every static feasible singleton replacement needs an
unloaded core anchor. For the primitive V=B shell the entire interior
singleton route is excluded, regardless of new contact count. Collective
replacement, changed anchors and strict sub-D constructions remain open.

## Sparse collective packets with only loaded core anchors

Extended the static singleton exclusion to actual collective packets.
Retained isolates are free variables, so no generic pin assumption is
needed. A positive pinned stress component with at most J new contacts
has loaded pins of diameter at most J. Canonical midpoint concentration
